{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And you.</b> The sense of אך (only): because I have commanded you, as written above. Perhaps they thought to do so on the Sabbath. Said one of the great sages: it is known that one Sabbath is the Sabbath of YHWH. What then is the sense of שבתותי (my Sabbaths)? And he answered: because two thousand years is the duration of the world — behold, the seventh thousand is Sabbath; also the year of Shemittah is Sabbath. Many Sabbaths [occur] in every year. And know that we do not find שבת stated explicitly in the festivals except as written, and there is a dispute over the meaning of ממחרת השבת (Leviticus 23:15). The truth is the words of the received tradition, except they require correction, as I will explain in its place. And the Day of Atonements is called Sabbath, but for Israel, not for the Name." "<b>For it is a sign.</b> At the end I will explain what the sign is." "<b>Who sanctifies you.</b> In your sanctifying the Sabbath which I sanctified, and I did no work on the day of the Sabbath."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase שתא אלפי שני הוה עלמא (six thousand years is the world) is a known rabbinic cosmological formula; translated literally here. The phrase 'the words of the received tradition' (דברי הקבלה) likely refers to the halakhic tradition interpreting ממחרת השבת as the day after the first day of Passover."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:39:41.428708317Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}